2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411157Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411340Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411407Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411492Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411560Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411646Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411711Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411802Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411876Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5411956Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412020Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412092Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412172Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412248Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412322Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412393Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412470Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412541Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412618Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412753Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412829Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412902Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5412982Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413046Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413273Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413418Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413488Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413565Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413649Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413730Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413807Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5413970Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414064Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414190Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414261Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414333Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414901Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5414967Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415034Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415108Z     Features
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415180Z     --------
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415252Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415615Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415684Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415759Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5415994Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416079Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416145Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416351Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416488Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416555Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416801Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416887Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5416954Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5417033Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5417106Z     
2026-09-23T07:02:27.5417468Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
